# Persistence Contract (Adopter Template)

Copy this contract into a project's own documentation. It describes a reusable pattern; it is not
an AIKit knowledge-base document.

## Version Matrix

| Stored version | Action |
|---|---|
| absent | Refuse as "not this project's state", or accept it only through a documented legacy migration |
| older | Migrate, or refuse with a clear Why/Fix when no migration exists |
| equal | Load |
| newer | Refuse; never quarantine, overwrite, or silently fall back to an older `.bak` |

A version mismatch is incompatibility, not corruption. Leave the file byte-identical.

## Write Order

1. Check that at least 10 MB are free.
2. Write a temporary file in the same directory; flush and fsync it.
3. Rotate the current valid file to `<name>.bak`.
4. Replace the primary with `os.replace`, retried up to five times.
5. Fsync the directory (a no-op on Windows).
6. On failure, delete the temporary file and raise an error carrying Why and Fix; never report a
   successful save when the replace did not land.

## Load Order

1. Parse the file. Invalid UTF-8 or JSON is corruption: quarantine it and try `.bak`.
2. Validate the version before normalizing or migrating anything.
3. Migrate in memory. A read-only command (`status`, `resume`, `audit`) never rewrites a valid
   state file; a version refusal leaves it byte-identical.
4. Recover from `.bak` only when its own version is supported.

## Adapter Boundary

Keep `to_payload()` and `from_payload()` in the project's domain layer. The atomic-write,
backup, quarantine, version-check and migration machinery is the reusable part.
